  "source": {
    "url": "https://external-secrets.io/latest/introduction/stability-support/",
    "ref": "external-secrets/external-secrets @ v2.14.0 (e932f3a3f, 2026-10-23; clone HEAD bcffd7b9b) read at the last GA tag of every minor v0.8.7 … v2.14.0 plus the GitHub release bodies v0.9.0 … v2.14.0",
    "lastVerified": "2026-11-09",
    "note": "supportWindows is deliberately NULL although docs/introduction/stability-support.md publishes a 'Supported Versions' table, because that table is not a compatibility window: its 'Kubernetes Version' column is the version(s) upstream's own test suite ran ('We do not do test coverage for any other kubernetes version than the ones running on our test suites.'), and since 0.14 most rows name ONE version (2.14 -> 1.37, 2.9-2.13 -> 1.36, 0.16 -> 1.32). Transcribed as min..max it would tell a 1.36 cluster that ESO 2.14 'requires Kubernetes 1.37 or newer' and every 1.34 cluster on 2.9-2.13 that ESO 'does not support' it — claims the vendor never made. The table's 'End of Life' column is equally a cadence fact, not a dated support promise: each minor ends the day the next ships ('Any other minor version releases are automatically deprecated as soon as a new minor version comes'). So the vendor's statement is transcribed as what it is — a support POLICY, 'external-secrets only supports the most-up-to date, current minor version' — as supportedMinorsBehindLatest=0 (an installed minor below 2.14 gets the MEDIUM outside-vendor-support finding), and the forced hop is the opt-in CURRENCY hop (currencyHop=true) to latestKnownVersion 2.14.0, the newest GA tag; every surface says it is NOT required by the Kubernetes upgrade. NEVER derive a window from go.mod. The page also says 'We strongly recommend upgrading one minor version at a time', which the generated rules cannot enforce and the currency hop does not claim. rules and upgradeNotes are EMPTY BY DESIGN: the breaking changes live in the generated-rule pipeline (scripts/readiness-agent/kb/external-secrets -> src/main/resources/k8s-rules/external-secrets), extracted mechanically by extract-external-secrets-notes.py from FIVE upstream paths because ESO publishes no CHANGELOG: (A) the GitHub release bodies' hand-written preamble ('# BREAKING CHANGE', '# Guide to Promoting to 0.16') kept whole plus upgrade-relevant generated PR titles (committed snapshot under kb/external-secrets/upstream/), (B) docs deprecation lines new per minor, (C) an API ledger (CRD served/storage versions, removed provider keys and engineVersion values, '// Deprecated:' markers, provider maintenance status), (D) controller flags removed / re-defaulted, (E) Helm chart values removed / re-defaulted. Tags, not branches: ESO cut no release branch after release-0.9. Floor 0.9 (Jun 2023); an older install reports VERSION_NOT_IN_CATALOG rules-wise and still gets the support-policy finding. Detection: the controller, webhook and cert-controller all run the same image, ghcr.io/external-secrets/external-secrets (also served as oci.external-secrets.io/external-secrets/external-secrets from 0.10.4), with -ubi / -ubi-boringssl tag variants; Bitnami's repackaged bitnami/external-secrets is the same operator. The retired Kubernetes External Secrets project (external-secrets/kubernetes-external-secrets, godaddy/kubernetes-external-secrets) is a different product and does NOT match. The operator's own CRs (ExternalSecret, ClusterExternalSecret, SecretStore, ClusterSecretStore, PushSecret, ClusterPushSecret) are collected by the agent arm (org toggle external-secrets; key names, enums, presence and names only — never a secret value or a remote key path); inventoryKinds stays EMPTY because the generated rules read those rows through the evaluator, not through the legacy per-kind CR fetch this field drives."
  },
